     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, <div>on the apply documentation we can read:</div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\">This function is very similar to </span><span style=\"display:inline;border-style:none;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\"><code style=\"font-size:1em;font-family:&#39;andale mono&#39;,&#39;lucida console&#39;,monospace;line-height:1.5\"><a href=\"http://inside-r.org/r-doc/base/apply\" style=\"color:rgb(0,119,223)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://inside-r.org/r-doc/base/apply&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792357903468000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1oi7hXxUxFV2BzM55QKXio\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,51,153);font-weight:bold\">apply</span></a></code></span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\">, except that it will always return an array, and when the function returns &gt;1 d data structures, those dimensions are added on to the highest dimensions, rather than the lowest dimensions.</span><br></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\">I don&#39;t understand why. I mean, I wanted to replace my apply with aaply but now I would deal with the change in the order of the dimensions. I apply a sort of aggregation with a tapply on the first dimension of a 3d array passing from the dimensions c(1000, 20, 30) to c(100, 20, 30). If I replace the `apply` with `aaply` I finally get an array with the dimensions c(20, 30, 100). It will be easy to fix it but I am curious on the reasons of this behaviour. </span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\">Thanks again and thanks to plyr that is making my job easier and more entertaining, </span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Helvetica Neue&#39;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:19.6000003814697px\">Matteo</span></div></div>"
